1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:05,324
The brush uncovers gold again — but this coin is different from the Kushana one.

2
00:00:05,324 --> 00:00:10,157
It is finer, the design is more carefully composed, and the legend is in Sanskrit.

3
00:00:14,733 --> 00:00:19,867
Somebody has decided that a coin should be a statement about a king, not just a weight of metal.

4
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:26,768
Inscriptions, above all one remarkable pillar inscription.

5
00:00:26,768 --> 00:00:30,155
Coins, which the Guptas issued in gold in real variety.

6
00:00:30,155 --> 00:00:37,410
And travellers' accounts, including a Chinese pilgrim who walked across northern India and wrote down what he saw.

7
00:00:41,767 --> 00:00:43,520
The dynasty begins small.

8
00:00:43,520 --> 00:00:47,229
Shri Gupta and Ghatotkacha are little more than names.

9
00:00:47,229 --> 00:00:54,917
Chandragupta the First, from about 319 or 320 CE, is where it becomes a kingdom — and his marriage to Kumaradevi,

10
00:00:54,917 --> 00:00:59,668
a Lichchhavi princess, mattered enough that coins commemorate it.

11
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:07,452
The Gupta era is counted from his accession, which is how we date much of what follows.

12
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:16,813
Then Samudragupta, and almost everything we know about him comes from one source:

13
00:01:16,813 --> 00:01:22,689
an inscription carved on an Ashokan pillar at Allahabad, composed by his court poet Harishena.

14
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:32,465
It is a praise-poem, a prashasti, and it lists his campaigns in categories — kings of the north uprooted,

15
00:01:32,465 --> 00:01:39,962
kings of the south defeated and then reinstated, frontier states made tributary, and distant powers that sent envoys.

16
00:01:43,033 --> 00:01:45,627
Two details about him that examiners like.

17
00:01:45,627 --> 00:01:49,125
His coins include a type showing the king playing a vina,

18
00:01:49,125 --> 00:01:54,552
a stringed instrument — a ruler advertising himself as a musician, which is unusual.

19
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:01,535
And he performed the ashvamedha, the horse sacrifice,

20
00:02:01,535 --> 00:02:07,288
an old ritual claim to paramountcy that had fallen out of use and which he deliberately revived.

21
00:02:11,033 --> 00:02:14,131
Chandragupta the Second, who took the title Vikramaditya,

22
00:02:14,131 --> 00:02:19,311
extended the empire west by defeating the Western Kshatrapas — the Shaka rulers we met two episo

23
00:02:19,311 --> 00:02:24,845
des ago — which gave the Guptas the ports of the west coast and the trade that went through them.

24
00:02:29,467 --> 00:02:33,942
He issued silver coinage for those western provinces, following local custom.

25
00:02:33,942 --> 00:02:37,728
His court is where the tradition of the nine gems, the navaratna,

26
00:02:37,728 --> 00:02:42,274
is located, though that tradition is later and not contemporary evidence.

27
00:02:44,867 --> 00:02:49,430
The Allahabad inscription is our main source and it is also, by design, flattering.

28
00:02:49,430 --> 00:02:53,695
One reading takes its account of the southern campaign fairly literally.

29
00:03:09,300 --> 00:03:12,417
Another points out that a prashasti exists to glorify,

30
00:03:12,417 --> 00:03:18,876
that reinstating defeated kings is not the same as ruling them, and that the Guptas never administered the south.

31
00:03:18,876 --> 00:03:23,920
Most historians accept the campaigns happened and are cautious about what they achieved.

32
00:03:23,920 --> 00:03:27,506
Use the inscription, and say what kind of document it is.

33
00:03:30,100 --> 00:03:33,047
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

34
00:03:33,047 --> 00:03:38,521
Chandra, Samudra, Vikramaditya — the first three that matter, in order.

35
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:44,678
Dig Quiz.

36
00:03:44,678 --> 00:03:45,966
Pause and name it.

37
00:03:45,966 --> 00:03:54,467
First: the praise-poem inscribed on an Ashokan pillar, composed by Harishena, which is our main source for Samudragupta.

38
00:03:58,633 --> 00:04:00,357
The Allahabad prashasti.

39
00:04:00,357 --> 00:04:05,805
Second: the instrument Samudragupta is shown playing on one of his coin types.

40
00:04:05,805 --> 00:04:06,795
The vina.

41
00:04:09,367 --> 00:04:12,888
Third: the title taken by Chandragupta the Second.

42
00:04:12,888 --> 00:04:13,854
Vikramaditya.

43
00:04:13,854 --> 00:04:19,332
In Mains, a frequent question asks how reliable prashastis are as sources.

44
00:04:23,233 --> 00:04:31,169
Say what they are for, what they reliably give you — names, titles, the self-image of a court — and what they will not give you.

45
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:39,671
A Gupta coin takes its place in the gallery, designed as much to be looked at as to be spent.

46
00:04:43,600 --> 00:04:51,605
Next gallery: how the empire was actually governed — and a change in how land was held that outlasts the dynasty by centuries.

47
00:04:51,605 --> 00:04:55,498
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

